A||N||5||burdell1||Touching up Bumper Paint||||5
Z||000000||burdell1||11-05-1999||03:15 PM||||Awhile back I got into a fender bender with my '85 Fiero and a little space (around 2" x 3") was left bare...The red paint came right off....Since I cannot afford at the moment to get it repainted in a shop, I thought I could buy some Touchup paint spray and paint it myself. Any tips? Would a airbrush work better?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||11-05-1999||05:02 PM||||I'm getting ready to have my car painted and while talking to painters they told me not to put any lacquer on the car. I believe that all the touchup paints are lacquer. How about it paint shop guys?||||reg||1
Z||000002||trackerrrr||11-05-1999||11:14 PM||||I would use the touchup as a temp fix if it matches close enough.  Most store bought touchup paint won't though because your paint/clearcoat will have some deterioration anyway.  Phil, I don't think any of the touchups are lacquer anymore.  It fades quickly and is very brittle, won't flex with the body panels.  Lacquer is becoming illegal to paint with anyway, at least in California it is now.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ggarsk||11-05-1999||11:20 PM||||Try the Dupli-Color line of touch up paints. I know that the Artic White is a perfect match for my white Fiero. All their touch up paints are supposed to be direct matches to factory. <p>[This message has been edited by ggarsk (edited 11-06-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||11-06-1999||10:28 AM||||As a tribute to Cliff and the great job he does with this forum, I think you should paint your bumper with "Dutch Boy".<P>LOL||||reg||10
Z||000005||burdell1||11-06-1999||11:20 AM||||It would be kind of ironic since I am always on the lookout for cute Dutch girls....||||reg||6
